\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Post-training protocol and RLVR}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{post-training}
\tag{rlvr}
\tag{reinforcement-learning}

\p{A \strong{post-training protocol} #{P} starts from a base policy
#{\pi_{\theta_0}} and specifies a training task law, a rollout procedure, a
feedback source, a reward or loss design, a credit rule, an update rule, and a
stopping rule under a lifecycle budget. Its result is generally random: the
trained policy depends on sampled tasks, generations, environment events,
verifier outcomes, and optimizer state.}

\p{\strong{Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards} (RLVR) is the
special case in which at least one load-bearing reward component is computed
from independently checkable evidence: for example an exact answer, unit test,
proof checker, simulator state, or sandbox outcome. For a sampled trajectory
#{\tau}, the verifier produces evidence #{e(\tau)} and a reward
#{R(\tau,e(\tau))}. Group-based methods compare several trajectories for the
same task before assigning advantages and updating the policy.}

\p{The following diagram is \strong{schematic}. It separates information
creation in rollouts and environments from the later reuse of that information
by an optimizer.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=8mm,
  every node/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center,
    text width=27mm, minimum height=11mm},
  every path/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node (task) {sample task};
\node[right=of task] (roll) {policy rollout\\or agent trace};
\node[right=of roll] (verify) {verifier and\\environment evidence};
\node[right=of verify] (credit) {reward, credit,\\proposed update};
\node[below=of credit] (policy) {updated policy};
\draw (task) -- (roll);
\draw (roll) -- node[draw=none, above=5mm, text width=20mm] {rollout cost} (verify);
\draw (verify) -- node[draw=none, above=5mm, text width=20mm] {signal} (credit);
\draw (credit) -- (policy);
\draw[bend right=38] (policy.west) to
  node[draw=none, below=6mm, text width=27mm] {next round or reuse} (roll.south);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The general reinforcement-learning semantics are described in
\citef{murphy2024reinforcement}. The current language-model instantiations are
exemplified by \citef{shao2024deepseekmath}, \citef{guo2025deepseek}, and
\citef{yu2025dapo}. The definition is broader than any one optimizer: GRPO,
PPO, policy-gradient variants, rejection sampling, and hybrid objectives can
all instantiate different fields of #{P}.}
